"The surah begins with the letters Alif, Lam, Mim, Ra. Although these letters may seem meaningless on their own, they are the fundamental elements that constitute the verses of the Quran, the Book of Allah. When brought together properly, these letters become clear proofs that manifest the meanings of the Book of truth. What has been revealed to you from Allah is entirely the truth; yet most people do not believe.\n\nIt is Allah Who raised the heavens without pillars that you can see. Then He established Himself above the Throne (istiwa) and subjected the sun and the moon. Each runs its course for an appointed term. He governs all affairs and explains the verses in detail so that you may believe with certainty in the meeting with your Lord. In explaining the raising of the heavens without pillars in this verse, Elmalılı touches upon old and new astronomical views, ultimately drawing attention to divine power. Furthermore, he emphasizes that this verse constitutes proof of Allah's existence through observation and reason.\n\nIt is He Who spread out the earth far and wide, placed therein mountains and rivers, created pairs of every kind of fruit, and draped the night over the day. Indeed, in that are signs for a people who reflect. And on the earth are neighboring tracts of land, vineyards, crops, and date palms, all watered with the same water, yet He makes some of their fruits superior to others. Indeed, in that are signs for a people who use their intellect.\n\nIf you are astonished, what is truly astonishing is the disbelievers' saying: 'When we have become dust, will we really be created anew?' These are the ones who have disbelieved in their Lord; there are iron shackles around their necks, and they are the companions of the Fire, abiding therein eternally. They challenge you to hasten the evil before the good, even though exemplary punishments have already passed before them. Your Lord is full of forgiveness for people despite their wrongdoing, but His punishment is also severe.\n\nThe disbelievers say, 'Why has a sign not been sent down to him from his Lord?' You are only a warner, and for every people there is a guide. Allah knows what every female carries, and what the wombs lose or increase; everything with Him is by due measure. He is the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, the Grand, the Most High. It is all the same whether one conceals speech or speaks openly, and whether one hides by night or walks out by day. For each person there are angels following closely before and behind him, guarding him by Allah's command. Allah does not change the blessing He has bestowed upon a people until they change what is in themselves. When Allah wills adversity for a people, there is no turning it back, and they have no protector besides Him.\n\nAllah raised the heavens without visible pillars and spread out the earth. The terrestrial globe was detached from the sun by Allah's command, and firm mountains, rivers, and pairs of every kind of fruit were created upon it. These pairs were created as male and female, bearing fruit through pollination. Although the science of botany discovered this only later, the Quran announced this truth centuries before. This fact is clear proof that the Quran is the Word of Allah.\n\nAdjacent tracts of land on earth possess different characteristics; some are barren, while others are fertile. Vineyards, crops, and date palms watered by the same water produce yields of different flavors. This diversity demonstrates that it does not occur through nature on its own, but by Allah's power. Those who use their intellect realize from these proofs that Allah is capable of creating anew. The deniers, on the other hand, utter what is truly astonishing by saying, 'When we have decayed and turned into dust, will we really be created anew?' Yet it is not re-creation that is astonishing, but rather denying it.\n\nThe deniers demand a verse (miracle) from the Prophet. Yet the numerous verses sent to them were already sufficient. The Prophet is merely a warner; for every people there is a guide. Allah knows what every female carries, and what the wombs lose or increase. Everything with Him is by due measure. It is Allah Who knows all that is hidden and manifest, and Who is greater and more exalted than all things.\n\nFor every person there are guardian angels before and behind him, protecting him by Allah's command. Allah does not change the condition of a people until they change what is in themselves. And when He wills adversity for a people, there is no turning it back, and they have no protector besides Allah.\n\nAllah shows you the lightning to inspire fear and hope, and brings forth heavy, rain-laden clouds. The thunder glorifies Him with praise, and the angels glorify Him in awe of Him. He sends thunderbolts and strikes whom He wills. True supplication is to Him alone; those invoked besides Him cannot respond to anything at all. Whoever is in the heavens and on earth prostrates to Allah, willingly or unwillingly, as do their shadows in the mornings and afternoons.\n\nIn the thirteenth verse, it is recounted that thunder and lightning glorify Allah with praise, and that the angels glorify Him in awe of His majesty. Then, the account of Arbad ibn Rabi'ah and 'Amir ibn al-Tufayl seeking to assassinate the Prophet (peace be upon him) and Allah punishing them is mentioned: 'Amir contracted the plague and died while fleeing, while Arbad perished from being struck by a thunderbolt.\n\nIn the fourteenth verse, with the expression 'To Him alone is the true supplication,' it is emphasized that praying to anyone other than Allah is futile. Idols, saints (awliya), or other beings cannot respond to any prayer on their own. Through a philosophical explanation, it is noted that beings are distinct from one another, and that one can influence another only by Allah's leave. Just as a thirsty person reaches out for water, any supplication not directed to Allah is in vain.\n\nIn the fifteenth verse, it is reported that every being in the heavens and on earth prostrates to Allah, willingly or unwillingly, and that even shadows are included in this prostration. This demonstrates that everything submits to Allah's command.\n\nIn verses 16-17, the polytheists are asked: Who is the Lord of the heavens and the earth? It is certainly Allah. Taking others besides Him as protectors is sheer blindness. Then a parable is given: Allah sends down water from the sky, valleys flow with torrents, and the flood carries scum on its surface. A similar scum forms when metals are melted. This scum is cast away, while what is beneficial remains. Such is truth and falsehood; even if falsehood seems apparent, it vanishes, while the truth is enduring.\n\nIn the eighteenth verse, it is conveyed that for those who respond to their Lord is the best reward (Paradise), while those who do not respond would want to offer everything on earth as ransom, yet it will not be accepted; for them awaits a terrible reckoning and Hell.\n\nVerses 19-22 enumerate the qualities of people of understanding (ulu al-albab): they fulfill Allah's covenant, maintain the ties of kinship (silat al-rahim), fear their Lord, dread the terrible reckoning, remain patient, establish prayer, spend in charity (infaq), and repel evil with good. These are the ones who attain the good outcome of the ultimate abode.\n\nIn verses 23-24, glad tidings are given that these believers will enter the Gardens of Eden ('Adn) together with the righteous among their parents, spouses, and offspring, and that the angels will enter upon them from every gate, saying, 'Peace be upon you for what you patiently endured; how excellent is the final home!'\n\nIn the twenty-fifth verse, it is stated that upon those who break the covenant of Allah, sever what He has ordered to be joined, and spread corruption on earth, there is a curse and the wretched home (Hell). Although this verse was revealed concerning the People of the Book (Ahl al-Kitab), its ruling is general.\n\nIn the 26th verse, it is recalled that Allah expands provision for whom He wills and restricts it, that the disbelievers rejoice in the worldly life, whereas the worldly life, compared to the Hereafter, is nothing but a brief provision for a journey.\n\nIn verses 27–29, it is stated that the disbelievers say, 'If only a sign were sent down to him from his Lord,' to which it is answered that Allah leaves astray whom He wills and guides to Himself whoever turns to Him. It is emphasized that for those who believe, whose hearts find rest in the remembrance of Allah, and who perform righteous deeds, there is a good end (Paradise).\n\nTurning to Allah and repenting in submission is called inabah. The prerequisite of guidance is to break free from the desires of the soul and submit to the will of Allah. Every human being is granted the opportunity to choose guidance throughout their lifetime. If a person does not choose guidance during this period, misguidance becomes an inevitable habit.\n\nIn the verse, it is stated: 'They are the believers whose hearts find satisfaction with the remembrance of Allah.' Here, dhikrullah (remembrance of Allah) refers to the Quran, because the Quran is Allah's clearest proclamation. Hearts find peace only through the remembrance of Allah; since everything other than Him is transient, nothing else can provide satisfaction.\n\nThe remembrance of Allah is of two degrees: the first is actual guidance created directly in the heart; the second is reminding through verses and proofs. The Quran consists of rational verses valid for all times and everyone. Hearts that are not satisfied with it cannot find satisfaction in any miracle.\n\nThose who believe and perform righteous deeds are, as it were, the very heart of the entire universe. 'Tuba', the glad tiding given to them, is a tree in Paradise or a prayer meaning 'pleasantness and beauty.' This signifies a sweet life and continuous goodness.\n\nO Muhammad! We have sent you to a community before whom many communities had passed away, so that you may recite to them what We have revealed to you of the Quran while they disbelieve in the Most Merciful (al-Rahman). Say: 'He is my Lord; there is no deity except Him; in Him I have put my trust, and to Him is my return.'\n\nThe Quran is such a miracle that if mountains were moved, the earth were torn asunder, or the dead were made to speak, it would be by this Quran. Yet its true wisdom lies in its being recited and contemplated upon. Indeed, the polytheists demanded that the Prophet (peace be upon him) move the mountains and resurrect their ancestors, whereupon this verse was revealed.\n\nMessengers before you were also mocked; I granted them respite, then I punished them. It is Allah who oversees what every soul does. Yet they associate partners with Him; say: 'Name your partners! Or would you inform Allah of something He does not know?' The schemes of the disbelievers have been made alluring to them.\n\nThe description of the Paradise promised to the righteous (muttaqun): Rivers flow beneath it, its fruits and shade are everlasting. The end of the disbelievers is the Fire. Some of those who were given the Scripture rejoice in the Quran, while others reject parts of it. Say: 'I have only been commanded to worship Allah and not associate any partner with Him.'\n\nOur Prophet (peace be upon him) conveys that he was commanded to worship Allah, not to associate any partner with Him, and that he invites only unto Him. He says: 'O People of the Book, come to a word common between us and you: that we worship none but Allah, associate nothing with Him, and do not take one another as lords besides Allah.' Thus, the Quran was revealed as an Arabic judgment (hukm) that both enables the People of the Book to unite upon fundamental principles and expounds the details. This Book prevails over previous scriptures and must not merely be recited, but acted upon accordingly. Moreover, the original Arabic text of the Quran is the source of rulings; legal judgments cannot be derived directly from its translations. If you were to follow their desires after the knowledge that has come to you, you would have neither protector nor defender against Allah.\n\nThe messengers before you also had wives and children. This does not pose an obstacle to prophethood. The disbelievers objected by using the Prophet's humanity and his multiple marriages as an excuse. Yet prophets such as David (Dawud), Solomon (Sulayman), and Abraham (Ibrahim) had also married and had children. This objection effectively amounts to denying the previous prophets as well. Furthermore, no messenger can bring a miracle except by Allah's leave. For every appointed term there is a decree (kitab); that is, there is a designated time for what has been promised, and people are granted an opportunity within this duration. For this reason, different scriptures were revealed for different eras.\n\nAllah eliminates whatever He wills and confirms whatever He wills. This is true both in creation and in legislation. Abrogation (naskh, alteration of a ruling) is a requirement of Allah's wisdom. In previous divine laws, certain rulings were repealed and new ones were introduced. The Quran likewise supports the fundamental principles of earlier scriptures while abrogating some of their rulings. All of this is recorded in the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawh al-Mahfuz), the immutable mother book. Therefore, abrogation does not imply ignorance or subsequent realization on the part of Allah.\n\nO Muhammad! Whether We show you some of the punishment We have promised them or cause you to die, your duty is only to convey the message. Taking account belongs to Us. Do the disbelievers not see that We reduce the land from its borders? There is none who can hinder Allah's judgment, and He is swift in taking account. Those before them also plotted schemes, but all schemes belong to Allah. Every soul knows what it has earned. The disbelievers will come to know to whom the final outcome of this world belongs.\n\nThe disbelievers say, 'You are not a messenger sent by Allah.' Say: 'Allah is sufficient as a witness between me and you, and so are those who have knowledge of the Scripture.' Allah bears witness to my prophethood through clear proofs. Furthermore, the coming of this prophet was also foretold in the Torah and the Gospel. Fair-minded scholars who possess knowledge of these scriptures and do not follow their desires have also testified to this."